          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions covered here have a three-character code that takes no further character?",
          "answer": "The question doesn't name any conditions, and I can't see an earlier part of this conversation, so I don't know which conditions you mean. Could you tell me which ones you'd like checked?\n\nIn the meantime, here is how to recognise a code like this. The ICD-10-CM coding summary says a code is three to seven characters long. A decimal point follows the first three characters only when more characters follow. So a code with no decimal point, such as I10, has no further subdivision. Codes such as E11.9 have a decimal point and extra characters. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nSome related codes in the corpus do not fit this pattern. Asthma (J45) is never used at three characters. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (J44) has further subdivisions for complications. (Sources: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*; *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n\nIf you tell me the conditions, I can check each one against the coding guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
